<Docs title="Identity & scoped delegation"><div class="doc-title"><span class="section-kicker">IDENTITY / TRUST</span><h1>Authenticate. Map. Authorize.</h1><p>A valid credential identifies a caller. Your integration determines which Cedar principal that caller may represent.</p></div><h2>Standalone PDP authentication</h2><p>The server supports disabled authentication for local development and API-key bearer validation. ApiKeyStore persists Argon2id secret hashes with expiry and revocation state. There is no key-generation CLI command; use the Rust ApiKeyStore API.</p><p>The middleware checks the bearer key but does not bind the submitted subject to that key or enforce its scopes as Cedar policy. Only trusted application code should construct subjects and security-relevant context.</p><h2>JWT and OIDC libraries</h2><p>agentguard-auth provides JwtValidator for signature, issuer, audience, and time validation, with JWKS discovery/refresh support. OIDC discovery checks the configured issuer against returned metadata. The implementation supports EdDSA verification; do not infer support for every JWT algorithm from algorithm names in data types.</p><p>These are library APIs, not standalone server auth modes. Applications must call the validators and map validated claims into User or Agent identity. RFC 7519 / RFC 8725 and RFC 8414 describe the standards these components target.</p><h2>DPoP and workload identity</h2><p>DpopVerifier checks EdDSA proof signatures, the bound key thumbprint, HTTP method and URI, access-token hash, time, and replay state. The caller supplies the expected cnf.jkt from a validated token. It is not automatically invoked by HTTP API-key authentication.</p><p>With the spiffe feature, SpiffeValidator fetches an X.509-SVID using the SPIFFE Workload API and checks trust domains. SPIRE can provide that workload identity. This does not add automatic mTLS identity mapping to the standalone server.</p><h2>Console OIDC</h2><p>The optional frontend implements OIDC Authorization Code + PKCE sign-in. Signed sessions carry viewer/admin roles. Admin values must be configured explicitly; otherwise no user becomes admin. Missing mandatory OIDC/session configuration returns 503.</p><h2 id="delegation">Delegation is a separate boundary</h2><p>DelegationSigner mints Ed25519 JWS tokens with issuer, subject, audience, expiry, allowed_actions, resource_patterns, and optional actor/constraint claims. DelegationVerifier checks cryptographic signature, key ID, algorithm, time, and audience.</p><Snippet title="Mint a scoped grant" code={'agentguard delegate \\\n  --from \'Agent::"research"\' \\\n  --to \'Agent::"summarizer"\' \\\n  --actions ToolCall::repo_read \\\n  --resources \'Repository::demo\' \\\n  --ttl 300 --key-file delegation.key --out delegation.jws'}/><p>Provision delegation.key using the supported Ed25519 key format before minting; see <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-cli/src/commands/delegate.rs">the CLI key loader</a>. Never treat a signed token as sufficient authorization by itself.</p><ol><li>Authorize the parent to issue this grant.</li><li>Verify the token against a trusted key and expected audience.</li><li>Check the requested action, resource, and constraints against the grant.</li><li>Apply the effective Cedar policy and enforce the resulting decision.</li><li>Implement any required revocation state and sender binding in your integration.</li></ol><p>Tokens contain RFC 8693-style actor claims and an audience, but no OAuth token-exchange endpoint or RFC 8707 resource-parameter interface is exposed. There is no delegation-token revocation service. API-key revocation is a separate facility; do not confuse it with revoking delegated grants.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/tree/master/crates/agentguard-auth/src">authentication modules</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-core/src/delegation.rs">delegation verification</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/frontend/README.md">console configuration</a>.</p></Docs>
